we're joined today by Jessica Pico who  is a senior legal analyst for rewire a  daily online publication that covers  reproductive justice Jessica in many of  the discussions around the possible  repeal and/or replacement of the  Affordable Care Act there's not been a  lot of discussion at least in the  mainstream about the co-pays for  contraception under the birth control  provisions of the Affordable Care Act  and a lot of reproductive health related  elements so let's start there a little  bit I mean what what is that risk when  we talk about the wholesale repeal of  the Affordable Care Act when it comes to  reproductive health  well what's risked was at risk if we  talk about a wholesale repeal is nearly  everything on let's start with the birth  control benefit for example what it did  was create a fully functional  non-discriminatory coverage for the  first time in the health insurance  industry by allowing birth control to be  considered preventive medicine which it  is and as a result it became part of  essential health services which in which  are basically included in all packages  so that's one component of it it means  for the first time ever the people who  need access to contraception for  whatever reason can have it covered  through their insurance by no additional  cost or copay so they're already paying  that in their monthly deductibles then  there's the potential Medicaid repeal  and we don't talk about that in terms of  a reproductive justice issue but I think  it's really important to wrap that in  because it's not necessarily tied to  contraception specifically but it is  tied to the ability of people of low  income and the disability community  folks to gain access to comprehensive  reproductive health care generally and  comprehensive health care also and  finally there's one more provision in a  called section 1557 which requires that  healthcare providers including including  insurance companies provide  non-discriminatory health benefits  across the board and so this is largely  protected the LGBTQ community from  facing discriminatory care in say  Catholic hospitals so while we can talk  about the birth  benefit certainly is a huge component of  it I think it's important for people to  remember that there are a lot of  components to reproductive health that  are built into the ACA when we talk  about the cost of some of these services  or products when not subsidized or  covered by insurance what are we talking  about I mean I think there's a lot of  people in our audience some some of whom  may not know just based on on being men  and never having been on the purchasing  side of some of these products but also  there may be people who have not availed  themselves of these services recently  HHS Secretary Tom price seems to believe  that there isn't anybody who can't  afford birth control without it being  covered by insurance but what types of  costs are we talking about well I think  when we hear comments like there's not a  single person who can who can who can't  afford birth control what we're hearing  is that men presume that all birth  control is say for example condoms and  something that you can pick up for 10  dollars or so at your local Walgreens  presuming the pharmacist or the person  at the point-of-sale doesn't turn you  away because they have a religious  objection to handing out contraception  so I think those comments are first and  foremost grounded in an understanding  that men use contraception and it's easy  to use contraception the reality is  obviously women use contraception too  and it costs up significantly more money  broadly speaking if we were to talk  about an IUD which is one of the most  effective forms of long-acting  reproductive contraception and we are  talking about a minimum cost at about  eight hundred dollars out-of-pocket  sometimes more and depending on the  variety in ways in which the cost and  the insurance program gets mucked up  that can go even higher a package of  birth control can cost some women under  some plans as much as seventy-five  dollars a month and that may not sound  like a lot to somebody who makes two  hundred three hundred four hundred  thousand dollars but if you're our wage  worker let's say you are someone who  works at a hospital as a care attendant  you are an hourly wage worker or you  make a low salary fifty dollars a month  for a package of birth control pills is  a lot of money  eight hundred dollars at lea  for an IUD is even more and prohibitive  well that's where you get to what I  think is the sort of fundamental  hypocrisy and double standard and if  anything has exposed the fact that many  individuals don't care about double  standards it's sort of been the last six  months of the current administration but  putting that aside for a moment you have  by and large the same political  constituency that says that they are  against abortion in all cases or in  almost all cases wanting not to make  birth control more affordable and more  accessible which has been shown in study  after study to reduce the number of  abortions because it reduces the number  of pregnancies which gets me Jessica  ultimately believing that this is not  really just about preventing abortion  because if it were they would be making  a lot of these birth control and  contraceptives more available and more  accessible it's about people suffering  the consequences for what the right  considers to be immoral behavior isn't  that really what underlies this  inconsistency between on the one hand  saying we want no abortions and on the  other hand not wanting to do what has  been shown to reduce the number of  abortions sure I think that you hit on a  very important point which is that there  is absolutely immoral hypocrisy I would  add to that that there is built within  that moral hypocrisy of belief that  women shouldn't even be working outside  of the home or that are valued enough to  be worthy of non-discriminatory  treatment one of the things that we know  for a fact is that the gendered wage gap  is exacerbated by unintended pregnancies  or even intended pregnancies that bring  women in and out of the workforce and so  to the extent that women are unable to  control the reproductive autonomy to the  extent that anybody is able to control  their reproductive autonomy what that  does is also affect the way that they  can earn a living and the kinds of  earning and earnings that they can make  over the course of a lifetime and so  this is as much an economic attack on  the poor as it is a moral attack on from  certain extreme portions of Christianity  in this country now it seems as though  at least temporarily  the Obamacare repeal has has sort of  stalled putting aside a repeal of the  Affordable Care Act we know about some  of the other ways in which reproductive  health can be attacked and one is the  cutting of funding at the state level to  Family Planning clinics as one example  what are other ways outside of the  repeal of the Affordable Care Act that  access to reproductive health benefits  and contraception can be attacked well  we've seen a swarm of these at the state  level since 2010 whether they are  architectural restrictions like the one  struck down by the Supreme Court or  whether they are waiting periods that  demand patients go visit a doctor and  then wait twenty-four forty-eight or  eight or in some cases 72 hours before  going back to another to the to the  doctor to actually have the procedure so  that adds cost it adds time and distance  I mean if you've got to arrange for  child care if you have to take time off  from from work and then I think also we  have to pay attention to the attacks on  the front doors of the clinics because  they are rising it's enough that we have  patients navigate a myriad of  legislative restrictions on abortion  access but then in many cases we force  them and their companions to walk  through throngs of protesters that are  calling them terrible name calling them  [ __ ] calling them baby killers and and  sometimes these are you know patients in  need of completed miscarriage or  terminating a pregnancy that was very  much wanted or you know a pregnancy  Silver's and and yet at the same time  this matters not to the people who are  protesting the clinics they cannot be  the difference  yeah I've been covering that quite a bit  it's absolutely deplorable behavior from  from a free speech and censorship  perspective how can you manage that in  other words without infringing on one's  of right to be out in public and  speaking or holding a sign that they  want at what point do we do we have to  say well this there's here the gray area  here's where you're on one side of it  and here's where you're clearly on the  other side of just free speech in public  I think that's a really fair question  and I think you know rewire has released  a short documentary called  Karen chaos that talks and explores this  protest movement in the way it affects  the front door of clinics and in that  film is a focus on the only clinic in  Fargo North Dakota and the law  enforcement officer there and I think  that she does a really good job of  balancing as the film shows the protest  rights of those who oppose abortion and  the rights of doctors and providers and  patients and their companions to safely  and securely access their constitutional  right to an abortion I think the other  thing that's important to remember and  gets lost in the speech kind of context  and was mentioned by Justice Stevens and  one of his abortion related decisions  that for buffer zones they didn't quite  get struck down but that there is also  the right to be left alone and we've  seen this in some of the cases involving  say for example the Westboro Baptist  Church where there are protest rights  that you also as an individual have a  right to not be compelled to listen to  speech that you don't want to and I  think that this is an issue that we need  to revisit as we see an amplification of  these protestors and them and their  tactics getting again more radicalized  and more direct I don't think this is  the thing that will go away and so I  think it is time to revisit and reinsert  into the conversation the right to be  left alone because it exists in the  Constitution the First Amendment  recognizes it and it's an important  component all very very important issues  spanning really a lot of different types  of legal and cultural questions we've  been speaking with Jessica Pico senior  legal analyst for rewire which is a  daily online publication covering  reproductive justice Jessica thanks so  much for talking to me today thank you  so much for having me on
 

